s":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm treating the twin prisoner's dilemma as two copies of the same model running the same reasoning. Since their observations differ, the question is whether my function's output can point to the other copy's action when that copy receives a different input. Under the essay's rule, a dependence arrow requires the mechanism to evaluate my function on my own input, and the other copy evaluates on a different one, so its action would strictly depend on a different node.\n\nI'm working toward a fixed point between the two nodes, but the inputs are logically isomorphic up to renaming, so the reasoning is structurally symmetric. Since the computation on the other copy's input is the same computation, I expect the same output. Cooperating gives 2 versus 4, so I'll choose to pass.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8540,"completion_tokens":558,"total_tokens":9098,"cost":0.04532,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.04532,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.03416,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.01116},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":301,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}